---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import SeamCalc from '../../components/calculators/SeamCalc.astro';
import { getTool } from '../../data/tools';

const tool = getTool('seam-allowance-calculator');
const faqs = [
    { q: "What is the standard seam allowance?", a: "⅝ in (1.5 cm) is common for US woven garments; always check your pattern." },
    { q: "Is this full pattern grading?", a: "No—it is a linear helper. Professional grading uses measurement charts per grade rule." }
];
---

<ToolLayout tool={tool} answerFirst={"Cut measurement = finished measurement + (seam_allowance × number_of_seam_edges) + ease. For a 20 in finished width with ⅝ in seams on both sides, cut 21.25 in. The same tool applies simple linear pattern grading: new = base + (grade_per_size × sizes_away)."} faqs={faqs}>
  <SeamCalc slot="tool" />

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <p>Enter finished measurement, seam allowance, edge count, and ease. Optionally grade a base measurement up or down by size steps.</p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <strong>Key takeaways</strong>
    <ul>
      <li>Commercial patterns often include SA—do not double-add.</li>
<li>Knits may use ⅜ in SA.</li>
<li>Quilting commonly uses ¼ in.</li>
      <li>Verify critical projects with a swatch or scrap sample.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>The formula / method</h2>
  <p>cut = finished + edges×SA + ease; graded = base + grade×sizes_away.</p>

  <h2>Worked example</h2>
  <p>Finished 36 in bust panel equivalent with ⅝ in SA ×2 → 37.25 in cut. Grade +1 in for two sizes → 38 in.</p>

  <h2>Tips</h2>
  <ul><li>Commercial patterns often include SA—do not double-add.</li>
<li>Knits may use ⅜ in SA.</li>
<li>Quilting commonly uses ¼ in.</li></ul>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul><li>Adding seam allowance to patterns that already include it.</li>
<li>Grading all measurements by the same rule (bust/waist/hip grade differently on real blocks).</li></ul>
  
    <h2>Units: inches, centimeters, yards, meters, grams, ounces</h2>
    <p>
      seam allowance calculator results support mixed unit workflows. Length inputs accept inches or centimeters where shown.
      Fabric and yarn outputs list yards and meters together (1 yd = 0.9144 m). Fiber weights list grams and ounces (1 oz ≈ 28.35 g).
      Keeping units explicit prevents the most common shopping mistakes when patterns, ball bands, and fabric bolts disagree.
    </p>
    <h2>Assumptions and limits</h2>
    <p>
      This tool is for planning. Stitch patterns, brand-specific yarn put-up, personal tension, shrinking, and finishing all move the real number.
      When a designer publishes tested yardage for a pattern, prefer that figure and use our estimate as a second opinion or substitution aid.
      Measurements assume the geometric models documented in the formula section—not every construction (e.g., circular yokes, bias skirts, or C2C blankets) matches a rectangle.
    </p>
    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      All math runs locally in your browser. We do not upload your project measurements. Shareable result links store state in the query string on your device’s URL only.
    </p>
  
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul><li><a href="https://extension.psu.edu/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Standard garment seam allowances — ⅝ in common in US home sewing</a></li></ul>
</ToolLayout>
